---

<DocsLayout
  title="Documentation — Tether CLI"
  description="Install Tether, connect and trust your machines, and manage dotfiles, packages, package security, profiles, and team sync."
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <section id="introduction">
    <p class="eyebrow">YOUR GUIDE TO A CONNECTED ENVIRONMENT</p>
    <h1>Get your machines<br /><em>on the same page.</em></h1>
    <p>
      Tether syncs dotfiles and packages across your machines through Git. It encrypts dotfiles by default and keeps package manifests readable.
    </p>
    <h3>Key Features</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Automatic Sync</strong> — Background daemon syncs changes every 5 minutes</li>
      <li><strong>End-to-End Encryption</strong> — AES-256-GCM encryption with passphrase-protected keys</li>
      <li><strong>Machine Profiles</strong> — Per-machine control over which dotfiles and packages sync</li>
      <li><strong>Project Configs</strong> — Sync .env files and IDE settings across machines by Git remote</li>
      <li><strong>Package Manager Support</strong> — Syncs Homebrew, npm, pnpm, bun, gem, and uv packages</li>
      <li><strong>Supply-Chain Checks</strong> — Synced packages install only from machines you trust, after release-age and OSV checks</li>
      <li><strong>Signed Machine Records</strong> — Each machine signs its package list with its own SSH key</li>
      <li><strong>Secret Detection</strong> — Scans for potential API keys and credentials before syncing</li>
      <li><strong>Git-Backed</strong> — Full version history and rollback capabilities</li>
      <li><strong>Team Secrets</strong> — Share encrypted secrets across teams with age encryption</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <hr class="my-12 border-surface" />

  <!-- Installation -->
  <section id="installation">
    <h2>Installation</h2>
    <h3>Homebrew (macOS and Linux)</h3>
    <Code code={`brew tap paddo-tech/tap
brew install tether-cli`} />
    <h3>Release download (Linux)</h3>
    <p>Each <a href="https://github.com/paddo-tech/tether-cli/releases">GitHub release</a> has a static Linux build for each CPU. These builds do not need a specific glibc, so they run on any distribution.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>x86_64:</strong> <code>tether-x86_64-unknown-linux-musl.tar.gz</code></li>
      <li><strong>aarch64:</strong> <code>tether-aarch64-unknown-linux-musl.tar.gz</code></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Download the tarball and its <code>.sha256</code> file. Check the checksum, then extract the <code>tether</code> binary to a directory on your <code>PATH</code>:</p>
    <Code code={`target=x86_64-unknown-linux-musl   # or aarch64-unknown-linux-musl
base=https://github.com/paddo-tech/tether-cli/releases/latest/download
curl -fLO "$base/tether-$target.tar.gz"
curl -fLO "$base/tether-$target.tar.gz.sha256"
sha256sum -c "tether-$target.tar.gz.sha256"
tar -xzf "tether-$target.tar.gz"
install -D -m 755 tether ~/.local/bin/tether`} />
    <p>Each tarball holds only the <code>tether</code> binary. The <code>latest</code> URL gets the newest stable release. For a specific release, use <code>releases/download/v&lt;version&gt;</code> instead.</p>
    <h3>From source</h3>
    <p>You need Rust 1.91 or later.</p>
    <Code code="cargo install --locked --git https://github.com/paddo-tech/tether-cli" />
    <h3>Requirements</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Platform:</strong> macOS or Linux (see <a href="#linux">Linux</a>)</li>
      <li><strong>curl:</strong> Used for OSV malware checks</li>
      <li><strong>Shell:</strong> zsh or bash</li>
      <li><strong>Git:</strong> Configured with GitHub, GitLab, or self-hosted Git server</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <hr class="my-12 border-surface" />

  <!-- Quick Start -->
  <section id="quick-start">
    <h2>Quick Start</h2>
    <p>Connect your first machine, then add the next one.</p>
    <h3>1. Initialize</h3>
    <Code code="tether init" />
    <p>Follow the prompts to configure your Git repository and encryption passphrase.</p>
    <h3>2. Verify Setup</h3>
    <Code code="tether status" />
    <p>You should see the daemon running and your synced files listed.</p>
    <h3>3. On Another Machine</h3>
    <Code code={`brew tap paddo-tech/tap && brew install tether-cli
tether init --repo git@github.com:username/dotfiles.git`} />
    <p>Enter the same passphrase. Your configs sync automatically.</p>
    <h3>4. Trust Your Machines</h3>
    <p>Each machine signs its package list with its own key. Packages from a machine you do not trust wait for approval. On each machine, read its own id and fingerprint:</p>
    <Code code="tether machines show" />
    <p>On your other machines, trust that machine with the fingerprint you read:</p>
    <Code code="tether machines trust <id> --fingerprint SHA256:..." />
    <p>See <a href="#machine-trust">Machine Trust</a> for details.</p>
  </section>

  <hr class="my-12 border-surface" />

  <!-- Upgrade -->
  <section id="upgrade">
    <h2>Upgrade to Tether 2.0</h2>
    <p>Tether 2.0 adds signed machine records, checks synced packages before it installs them, and gives packages to profiles. After the upgrade, each machine must trust your other machines once. Until then, packages from those machines wait in the Inbox.</p>

    <h3>Upgrade one machine at a time</h3>
    <p>You do not have to upgrade all machines at once. Machines on 1.11.10, 1.12.0 and 1.13.1 can read the synced files that 2.0 writes. Upgrade one machine, sync it, and trust it. Then do the next machine.</p>
    <p><code>tether status</code>, <code>tether machines list</code> and the dashboard Machines tab mark each machine that still runs 1.x as "on 1.x".</p>

    <h3>1. Upgrade a machine</h3>
    <Code code="brew upgrade tether-cli" />
    <p>If you installed a release download or built from source, install the new version the same way. See <a href="#installation">Installation</a>.</p>

    <h3>2. Sync it once</h3>
    <Code code="tether sync" />
    <p>The first sync makes the machine's signing key and signs its record.</p>

    <h3>3. Trust your upgraded machines</h3>
    <p>On each upgraded machine, read its id and full fingerprint:</p>
    <Code code="tether machines show" />
    <p>Then, on your other upgraded machines, trust it with that fingerprint:</p>
    <Code code="tether machines trust <id> --fingerprint SHA256:..." />
    <p>You can also approve the "new machine key" item when <code>tether sync</code> asks, or press <code>a</code> on the dashboard Security tab.</p>
    <p>A machine on 1.x has no signed record. Its packages wait in the Inbox as "from another machine". Upgrade that machine, sync it, and trust it. The next sync then installs its packages.</p>

    <h3>4. Review held packages</h3>
    <Code code={`tether packages inbox
tether packages approve --all --from <machine>   # after you review the list`} />
    <p>After you trust a machine, the next sync installs the packages its record lists. Packages that fail a check stay in the Inbox.</p>

    <h3>5. Check your settings</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Add third-party Homebrew taps that you use to <code>packages.brew.trusted_taps</code></li>
      <li>Add packages that need install scripts to <code>packages.allow_scripts</code></li>
      <li>Change <code>packages.min_release_age_days</code> if 7 days does not suit you</li>
      <li>Remove records that <code>tether machines list</code> marks as an old id of this machine</li>
      <li>Check that each machine has the profile you want. Packages now go only to the profiles that have them. See <a href="#package-profiles">Packages per profile</a></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Machines on 1.x during the upgrade</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>A machine on 1.x does not run the new checks. It still installs every synced package without them</li>
      <li>A machine on 1.x ignores profiles. It installs the packages of every profile</li>
      <li>A machine on 1.x can push its copy of <code>config.toml</code> back and forth with other machines after any config change. It can also revert one profile assignment. A sync on 2.0 then warns that this machine's profile changed, and names the command that restores it</li>
      <li>Set profiles before machines on 1.x join, or after they upgrade</li>
      <li>A 1.13.2 patch release for this config problem may follow. If it is available, upgrade machines that stay on 1.x to it</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>What changed</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Synced packages no longer install on their own unless a trusted machine record lists that exact version</li>
      <li>A machine installs only the packages of its own profile. Nothing is uninstalled. The first sync names packages that no longer come to this machine, with the command that shares them</li>
      <li>Packages from untrusted Homebrew taps now wait in the Inbox. Before, Tether skipped them with a warning</li>
      <li>Packages for managers that cannot enforce the release-age limit now wait in the Inbox. This covers gem and older npm, pnpm and bun</li>
      <li>Tether no longer runs <code>brew update</code>. Run it yourself to update Homebrew</li>
      <li><code>tether upgrade</code> asks first, lists what it changes, and never downgrades a package</li>
      <li><code>tether packages</code> now lists packages. <code>tether packages uninstall</code> replaces <code>remove</code>, which stays as an alias</li>
      <li><code>tether ignore secrets</code> and <code>tether ignore files</code> replace the old <code>tether ignore</code> forms, which still work</li>
      <li><code>tether machines rename &lt;NEW&gt;</code> renames only the machine it runs on</li>
      <li><code>-y</code> answers every prompt: it confirms, and takes the default answer of each question. Without a terminal, <code>approve</code> and <code>trust</code> still need <code>--expect</code> or <code>--fingerprint</code></li>
      <li>Every failed command exits with status 1 and prints one <code>Error:</code> line</li>
      <li>The Config tab no longer shows the <code>sync_versions</code> settings. Tether still writes them as <code>false</code>, because 1.x builds need them to read the synced config</li>
      <li>The dashboard shows a machine as online when its record is at most 90 minutes old</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <hr class="my-12 border-surface" />

  <!-- Linux -->
  <section id="linux">
    <h2>Linux</h2>
    <p>Tether runs on x86_64 and aarch64 Linux. Install it with Homebrew, a release download, or from source. See <a href="#installation">Installation</a>. Then run <code>tether init</code>.</p>

    <h3>Daemon</h3>
    <Code code="tether daemon install" />
    <p>On Linux, this installs and enables a systemd user service in <code>~/.config/systemd/user/tether.service</code>. The service gets your shell's <code>PATH</code>, <code>GEM_HOME</code>, and <code>GEM_PATH</code>. <code>tether daemon uninstall</code> removes it.</p>
    <p>Without a systemd user session, use <code>tether daemon start</code>.</p>
    <p>Paths with spaces, <code>$</code>, or <code>%</code> work. systemd cannot run a program whose path has a quote, a backslash, or a control character. In that case, <code>tether daemon install</code> stops with an error and writes no unit.</p>

    <h3>Notifications</h3>
    <p>Tether uses <code>notify-send</code> for desktop notifications. Without it, Tether logs this once and shows no notifications.</p>

    <h3>Packages</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Tether does not install Homebrew casks on Linux</li>
      <li>Tether skips gems that ship with Ruby or with Debian. On a Ruby from ruby-build or rbenv, Tether cannot identify these gems</li>
      <li>Machine records name their OS. When only Macs list a version, Linux tries it first. If it fails, Tether holds the newest release that suits Linux in the Inbox as "other OS version". Approve it to install exactly that release</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Merge tool</h3>
    <p>When the synced merge tool is <code>opendiff</code>, a machine without it uses <code>vimdiff</code>. To use another tool on one machine, set it in <a href="#local-config"><code>~/.tether/local.toml</code></a>.</p>
  </section>

  <hr class="my-12 border-surface" />

  <!-- How It Works -->
  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How It Works</h2>
    <h3>Architecture</h3>
    <p>
      Tether uses your Git repository to connect machines. The daemon detects changes and encrypts dotfiles before pushing them to Git.
    </p>
    <p>
      On your other machines, Tether pulls these changes and applies them automatically.
    </p>
    <h3>Sync Flow</h3>
    <FlowDiagram label="FROM YOUR MACHINE TO YOUR REPO" caption="Other machines pull these changes, decrypt the dotfiles, and apply them locally." steps={[
      { icon: 'file', title: 'Scan changes', detail: 'Check files and package manifests' },
      { icon: 'lock', title: 'Encrypt dotfiles', detail: 'Use your local encryption key' },
      { icon: 'git', title: 'Sync through Git', detail: 'Commit and push to your repo' },
    ]} />
    <ol>
      <li>Daemon detects file changes (every 5 minutes or manual <code>tether sync</code>)</li>
      <li>Secret scanner checks for credentials</li>
      <li>Files are encrypted (if enabled)</li>
      <li>Tether signs this machine's record of its packages</li>
      <li>Changes committed and pushed to Git</li>
      <li>Other machines pull and decrypt</li>
      <li>Other machines verify the record and check each new package before they install it</li>
    </ol>
    <h3>Data Layout</h3>
    <p>Local Tether data lives in <code>~/.tether/</code>:</p>
    <Code code={`~/.tether/
├── config.toml      # Configuration (versioned)
├── local.toml       # Settings for this machine only (never synced)
├── state.json       # Sync state (hashes, timestamps)
├── sync/            # Personal sync repo
├── teams/<name>/    # Team sync repos
├── collabs/         # Collab project configs
├── backups/         # File backups
├── identity.pub     # Age public key (for team secrets)
├── signing_key      # This machine's SSH signing key
├── trusted_keys     # Machine keys this machine trusts (never synced)
├── record_generations.json  # Newest record accepted per key (never synced)
├── machine.json     # This machine's last record (never synced)
├── inbox.json       # Approval inbox (never synced)
├── run/             # Empty working directory for package managers
├── daemon.pid       # Daemon process ID
├── daemon.log       # Daemon logs
└── conflicts.json   # Conflict state`} />
    <p>The sync repository structure:</p>
    <Code code={`sync/
├── profiles/<name>/ # Profile-specific dotfiles (encrypted)
├── dotfiles/        # Flat-layout dotfiles (legacy/shared)
├── configs/         # App configs (tether config, etc.)
├── projects/        # Project-local configs
├── manifests/       # Package manifests
├── packages/        # profiles.toml: the profiles each package belongs to
└── machines/        # Per-machine records (<id>.json) and signatures (<id>.json.sig)`} />
  </section>

  <hr class="my-12 border-surface" />

  <!-- Dashboard -->
  <section id="dashboard">
    <h2>Dashboard (TUI)</h2>
    <p>
      Run <code>tether</code> with no arguments to launch the interactive terminal dashboard.
    </p>
    <Code code="tether" />
    <p>The dashboard has six tabs. Switch tabs with the number keys, <code>Tab</code>, or a mouse click.</p>

    <h3>Tabs</h3>
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-surface">
          <th class="text-left py-2 pr-4">Tab</th>
          <th class="text-left py-2 pr-4">Key</th>
          <th class="text-left py-2">Description</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr class="border-b border-surface/50">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-xs">Overview</td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-xs">1</td>
          <td class="py-2">Dotfiles, packages, machines, recent activity, a chart of sync commits per day, and the daemon log</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-surface/50">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-xs">Files</td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-xs">2</td>
          <td class="py-2">Synced dotfiles with file history, diffs with line numbers, restore from a commit or a backup, and sharing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-surface/50">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-xs">Packages</td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-xs">3</td>
          <td class="py-2">Packages by manager with the profiles of each package, manifest history, failed installs, install, and uninstall</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-surface/50">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-xs">Machines</td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-xs">4</td>
          <td class="py-2">A card for each machine: id, profile, OS, Tether version, packages, last sync, online/idle/stale state, and key trust</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-surface/50">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-xs">Config</td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-xs">5</td>
          <td class="py-2">Inline config editor with toggles, validated text fields, list editing, and the package security settings</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-surface/50">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-xs">Security</td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-xs">6</td>
          <td class="py-2">The Inbox: held packages with their reasons, source machine, commit, and OSV advisories, machine keys that wait for trust, and the trusted machines</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>A machine is online when its record is at most 90 minutes old. An idle machine updates its record once an hour. After 24 hours without an update, the card shows the machine as stale. A machine that still runs 1.x shows "on 1.x".</p>
    <p>The header shows this machine, its profile, the daemon state, and a spinner while a sync runs. When items wait for approval, the header shows <code>⚑ Inbox &lt;count&gt;</code>. The badge is red when an item is malicious.</p>

    <h3>Themes</h3>
    <p>On a terminal with true color, the dashboard uses Catppuccin Mocha or Latte. It picks one from the terminal background. Other terminals get a 16-color theme. To choose a theme, set <code>dashboard.theme</code>:</p>
    <Code code="tether config set dashboard.theme mocha   # mocha, latte, ansi, or auto" />

    <h3 id="dashboard-keys">Keys</h3>
    <p>The footer shows the most important keys of the current tab. When it has no room for all of them, it shows <code>? more</code>. Press <code>?</code> to see every key of the current screen. You can also click a tab, a row, or a key hint, and scroll lists with the mouse wheel.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>Enter</code> opens: a section, a history, a diff, details, or a value to edit</li>
      <li><code>Esc</code> closes, collapses or goes back. It never quits. Press <code>q</code> or <code>Ctrl+C</code> to quit</li>
      <li><code>x</code> removes, uninstalls, rejects or untrusts. It always asks first</li>
    </ul>
    {keyTables.map(table => (
      <Fragment>
        <h4>{table.title}</h4>
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-surface">
              <th class="text-left py-2 pr-4 w-48">Key</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2">Action</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {table.keys.map(([key, action]) => (
              <tr class="border-b border-surface/50">
                <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-xs whitespace-nowrap">{key}</td>
                <td class="py-2">{action}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </Fragment>
    ))}

    <h3>Questions</h3>
    <p>These actions ask first: restore, rollback, uninstall, remove, reject, trust, untrust, approve all, stop the daemon, and an approval that needs a second look, such as a machine key, a failed signature, or a package that OSV could not check.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A question ignores keys for 0.4 seconds after it opens. Until then, it shows dim buttons with a countdown. A key that you typed for another screen cannot answer it</li>
      <li>After that, only <code>y</code> accepts</li>
      <li><code>n</code>, <code>Esc</code> and <code>Enter</code> cancel. A click on the tab under the question does not accept it</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <hr class="my-12 border-surface" />

  <!-- Commands -->
  <section id="commands">
    <h2>Commands</h2>
    <p>Run <code>tether &lt;command&gt; --help</code> for the options of each command.</p>

    <h3 id="cmd-scripting">Options for every command and for scripts</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><code>-y</code>, <code>--yes</code> — Answer every prompt: confirm the action, and take the default answer of each question. Without a terminal and without <code>-y</code>, a prompt fails with a message that names <code>-y</code></li>
      <li><code>-y</code> on its own never approves an Inbox item or trusts a key. Without a terminal, <code>packages approve</code> and <code>reject</code> need <code>--expect</code>, and <code>machines trust</code> needs <code>--fingerprint</code>. Only <code>packages approve --all</code> takes <code>-y</code> as its confirmation, and it still leaves machine keys in the Inbox. It does not pick an answer for a question that has no safe default</li>
      <li><strong>Exit status</strong> — 0 on success. A failed command prints one <code>Error: ...</code> line on stderr and exits with 1. An unknown command or option exits with 2</li>
      <li><strong>Colour</strong> — Tether prints no colour codes when stdout is not a terminal, such as a pipe or a file, or when <code>NO_COLOR</code> is set</li>
      <li><strong><code>--json</code></strong> — <code>status</code>, <code>machines list</code>, <code>packages list</code> and <code>packages inbox</code> print one JSON value on stdout. Messages go to stderr. The flag is experimental in 2.0: field names can still change</li>
      <li><strong>Machine arguments</strong> — Each command that takes a machine accepts its id, or a hostname that names exactly one machine</li>
      <li><strong>Package ids</strong> — A package id is <code>manager:name</code>, such as <code>npm:typescript</code>. Managers: <code>brew_formulae</code> (or <code>brew</code>), <code>brew_casks</code> (or <code>cask</code>), <code>brew_taps</code>, <code>npm</code>, <code>pnpm</code>, <code>bun</code>, <code>gem</code>, <code>uv</code></li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="cmd-dashboard">tether / tether dashboard</h3>
    <p>Launch the interactive TUI dashboard. See <a href="#dashboard">Dashboard</a> above for details.</p>
    <Code code="tether" />

    <h3 id="cmd-init">tether init</h3>
    <p>Initialize Tether on this machine.</p>
    <Code code="tether init [--repo <URL>] [--no-daemon] [--team-only]" />
    <ul>
      <li><code>--repo &lt;URL&gt;</code> — Git repository URL (interactive if omitted)</li>
      <li><code>--no-daemon</code> — Do not start the daemon after init</li>
      <li><code>--team-only</code> — Skip personal dotfiles and packages, and use team sync only</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="cmd-sync">tether sync</h3>
    <p>Manually trigger a sync.</p>
    <Code code="tether sync [--dry-run] [--force] [--rediscover]" />
    <ul>
      <li><code>--dry-run</code> — Show what would be synced. It ends with "Dry run: nothing changed"</li>
      <li><code>--force</code> — Skip conflict prompts</li>
      <li><code>--rediscover</code> — Ask again about file imports that you dismissed</li>
    </ul>
    <p>In a terminal, <code>tether sync</code> asks about packages and machine keys in the Inbox. The daemon holds them and sends one notification for each new batch.</p>

    <h3 id="cmd-status">tether status</h3>
    <p>Show the sync status, the daemon state, this machine's profile, and the synced files. It counts the items that wait in the Inbox and the files that have conflicts. It also names each machine that still runs 1.x.</p>
    <Code code="tether status [--json]" />

    <h3 id="cmd-diff">tether diff</h3>
    <p>Show differences between machines.</p>
    <Code code="tether diff [--machine <MACHINE>]" />
    <ul>
      <li><code>--machine &lt;MACHINE&gt;</code> — Compare with this machine (id or hostname)</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="cmd-daemon">tether daemon</h3>
    <p>Control the background daemon.</p>
    <Code code={`tether daemon start
tether daemon stop
tether daemon restart
tether daemon status
tether daemon logs [-f] [-n <LINES>]
tether daemon install
tether daemon uninstall`} />
    <ul>
      <li><code>status</code> — Show whether the daemon runs, whether its login service is installed, and the last sync and upgrade</li>
      <li><code>logs</code> — Print the end of the daemon log. <code>-n &lt;LINES&gt;</code> sets the number of lines (default 50). <code>-f</code> keeps printing lines as the daemon writes them</li>
      <li><code>install</code> — Install the login service: launchd on macOS, a systemd user service on Linux</li>
      <li><code>uninstall</code> — Remove the login service</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="cmd-machines">tether machines</h3>
    <p>List, show, trust and remove machines, and manage profiles.</p>
    <Code code={`tether machines list [--json]
tether machines show [MACHINE]
tether machines rename <NEW>
tether machines remove <MACHINE>
tether machines trust <MACHINE> [--fingerprint SHA256:...]
tether machines untrust <MACHINE>
tether machines profile <set|unset|create|edit|list>`} />
    <ul>
      <li><code>list</code> — List all machines with profile, version, last sync, key fingerprint, and trust state. It marks machines on 1.x, and records that may be an old id of this machine</li>
      <li><code>show [MACHINE]</code> — Show one machine: profile, versions, record status, full key fingerprint, and trust. Without a machine, it shows this machine</li>
      <li><code>rename &lt;NEW&gt;</code> — Rename this machine. Tether signs its record again under the new id. Other machines must trust the new id. The old form <code>rename &lt;OLD&gt; &lt;NEW&gt;</code> still works in 2.0 with a warning, and fails when OLD is not this machine</li>
      <li><code>remove &lt;MACHINE&gt;</code> — Remove a machine record and its trusted key on this machine. With <code>-y</code>, Tether removes it without a question and prints what it removed</li>
      <li><code>trust &lt;MACHINE&gt; --fingerprint SHA256:...</code> — Trust a machine's signing key if its fingerprint matches. Without a fingerprint, Tether shows the current one and asks, in a terminal only</li>
      <li><code>untrust &lt;MACHINE&gt;</code> — Stop trusting a machine's signing key</li>
      <li><code>profile set &lt;PROFILE&gt;</code> — Assign a profile to this machine. Each machine sets only its own profile</li>
      <li><code>profile unset</code> — Remove the profile assignment of this machine</li>
      <li><code>profile create &lt;PROFILE&gt; [--from &lt;PROFILE&gt;] [--managers &lt;LIST&gt;]</code> — Create a profile. In a terminal, it asks about each dotfile, folder and package manager. <code>-y</code> takes every default. <code>--from</code> copies a profile without questions. <code>--managers</code> sets its package managers, such as <code>brew,npm</code></li>
      <li><code>profile edit &lt;PROFILE&gt;</code> — Edit a profile</li>
      <li><code>profile list</code> — List all profiles and assignments</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="cmd-ignore">tether ignore</h3>
    <p>Manage secret scanning patterns, and files this machine keeps.</p>
    <Code code={`# Patterns the secret scanner skips
tether ignore secrets add <PATTERN>
tether ignore secrets list
tether ignore secrets remove <PATTERN>

# Files this machine keeps: a sync does not overwrite them
tether ignore files add <FILE>
tether ignore files project <PROJECT> <PATH>
tether ignore files list
tether ignore files remove <FILE>      # sync the file again`} />
    <p>The 1.x forms <code>ignore add|list|remove</code> and <code>ignore dotfile|project|sync-list|sync-remove</code> still work.</p>

    <h3 id="cmd-config">tether config</h3>
    <p>Manage configuration.</p>
    <Code code={`tether config get <KEY>
tether config set <KEY> <VALUE>
tether config edit
tether config dotfiles
tether config features              # List feature toggles
tether config features enable <F>   # Enable a feature
tether config features disable <F>  # Disable a feature`} />
    <ul>
      <li><code>get &lt;KEY&gt;</code> — Get a config value. Use nested keys such as <code>packages.brew.enabled</code></li>
      <li><code>set &lt;KEY&gt; &lt;VALUE&gt;</code> — Set a config value. Tether reads the value as TOML, so arrays work: <code>'["azure/kubelogin"]'</code>. A bare word stays a string. Tether refuses a key that the config does not have</li>
      <li><code>edit</code> — Open the config in your editor. It needs a terminal</li>
      <li><code>dotfiles</code> — Interactive UI for managing synced files</li>
      <li><code>features</code> — Manage feature toggles (see <a href="#feature-toggles">Feature Toggles</a>)</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="cmd-team">tether team</h3>
    <p>Manage team sync repositories, secrets, and project sharing.</p>
    <Code code={`# Team management
tether team setup                     # Interactive wizard
tether team add <URL>                 # Add team repo
tether team list                      # List teams
tether team switch <NAME>             # Switch active team
tether team status                    # Show status
tether team enable/disable            # Toggle sync
tether team remove [NAME]             # Remove team sync

# Organization mapping
tether team orgs add <ORG>            # Map GitHub org to team
tether team orgs list                 # List mapped orgs
tether team orgs remove <ORG>         # Unmap org

# Team secrets (age encryption)
tether team secrets add-recipient <KEY>     # Add member's public key
tether team secrets list-recipients         # List recipients
tether team secrets remove-recipient <NAME> # Remove a recipient
tether team secrets set <NAME>              # Set a secret
tether team secrets get <NAME>              # Get a secret
tether team secrets list                    # List all secrets
tether team secrets remove <NAME>           # Remove a secret

# Team files
tether team files list                # List synced team files
tether team files diff [FILE]         # Diff local and team version
tether team files reset [FILE]        # Reset to the team version
tether team files promote <FILE>      # Promote a local file to the team repo
tether team files ignore <FILE>       # Keep a file personal
tether team files unignore <FILE>     # Sync a file with the team again

# Project secrets
tether team projects add <FILE>       # Share project secret
tether team projects list             # List shared secrets
tether team projects remove <FILE>    # Remove a project secret
tether team projects migrate          # Auto-migrate to team
tether team projects purge-personal   # Remove personal copies`} />

    <h3 id="cmd-resolve">tether resolve</h3>
    <p>Resolve file conflicts.</p>
    <Code code="tether resolve [FILE]" />
    <p>Interactive resolution: keep local, use remote, merge, or skip.</p>

    <h3 id="cmd-unlock">tether unlock</h3>
    <p>Unlock encryption key with passphrase.</p>
    <Code code="tether unlock" />

    <h3 id="cmd-lock">tether lock</h3>
    <p>Clear cached encryption key.</p>
    <Code code="tether lock" />

    <h3 id="cmd-upgrade">tether upgrade</h3>
    <p>Upgrade installed packages to the newest release that the release-age limit allows.</p>
    <Code code="tether upgrade [--dry-run]" />
    <ul>
      <li>Tether lists what it will change and asks first. Without a terminal, it needs <code>-y</code></li>
      <li><code>--dry-run</code> lists what an upgrade would change, and which packages stay because the release-age limit allows only an older version. It changes nothing</li>
      <li>An upgrade never downgrades a package. It installs only a target newer than the installed version, at that exact version</li>
      <li>npm, pnpm, bun and uv upgrades skip releases newer than <code>packages.min_release_age_days</code></li>
      <li>Tether checks each npm, pnpm, bun, uv and gem upgrade target against OSV. A target with a <code>MAL-</code> advisory waits in the Inbox, and the installed version stays</li>
      <li>Tether asks before it upgrades a manager that cannot enforce the release-age limit. Without a terminal, or with <code>-y</code>, it skips that manager</li>
      <li>Homebrew upgrades include only outdated packages from trusted taps. Tether does not run <code>brew update</code>. Run it yourself to update Homebrew</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="cmd-history">tether history</h3>
    <p>Show the change history of a dotfile from the sync repo.</p>
    <Code code="tether history <FILE> [--limit <N>]" />

    <h3 id="cmd-restore">tether restore</h3>
    <p>Restore files from backup or git history.</p>
    <Code code={`tether restore list                  # List available backups
tether restore file                  # Interactive restore
tether restore file <FILE> --from <TS>  # Restore from a specific backup
tether restore git <FILE> [--commit <SHA>]  # Restore a dotfile from git history`} />

    <h3 id="cmd-rollback">tether rollback</h3>
    <p>Roll back a package manager's installed packages to a manifest commit. The managers are npm, pnpm, bun, gem and uv. Homebrew is not supported.</p>
    <Code code="tether rollback packages <MANAGER> <COMMIT>" />
    <p>Tether lists each package and version it would install, and asks before it changes anything. Without a terminal, it needs <code>-y</code>. A snapshot version other than the newest trusted version installs only when you confirm it in a terminal. <code>-y</code> does not confirm it. Packages that fail a check wait in the Inbox.</p>

    <h3 id="cmd-identity">tether identity</h3>
    <p>Manage age identity for team secrets encryption.</p>
    <Code code={`tether identity init     # Generate new age identity
tether identity show     # Show your public key
tether identity unlock   # Unlock with passphrase
tether identity lock     # Clear cached key
tether identity reset    # Generate new (destroys old)`} />

    <h3 id="cmd-packages">tether packages</h3>
    <p>List, install, share and uninstall packages, and decide on the Inbox.</p>
    <Code code={`tether packages [list] [--json]                       # List installed packages
tether packages install <ID>                          # Install a package another machine lists
tether packages share <ID> --to <PROFILES>            # Add profiles to a package
tether packages unshare <ID> --from <PROFILES>        # Take profiles off a package
tether packages uninstall [ID]                        # Uninstall here (alias: remove)
tether packages inbox [--json]                        # List held packages, taps, and keys
tether packages approve <ID> [--expect <VERSION|TAP|KEY>]
tether packages approve --all [--from <MACHINE>]
tether packages reject <ID> [--expect <VERSION|TAP|KEY>]`} />
    <ul>
      <li><code>list</code> — List installed packages by manager, with the profiles each belongs to and this machine's profile. A package that only this profile has shows "this profile only". It also shows failed installs. A bare <code>tether packages</code> lists too</li>
      <li><code>install &lt;ID&gt;</code> — Install a package that another machine lists, as the dashboard's Import does. OSV checks the release first. A package that waits in the Inbox needs <code>approve</code> instead</li>
      <li><code>share &lt;ID&gt; --to &lt;PROFILES&gt;</code> — Add profiles to the package, so machines in those profiles install it. Separate profiles with commas</li>
      <li><code>unshare &lt;ID&gt; --from &lt;PROFILES&gt;</code> — Take profiles off the package, so machines in those profiles stop installing it. Nothing is uninstalled. Machines keep any copy they have</li>
      <li><code>uninstall [ID]</code> — Uninstall the package here, and take this machine's profile off it. Other profiles keep it. Without a package, pick packages in a terminal. <code>remove</code> is an alias</li>
      <li><code>inbox</code> — List the Inbox: packages, taps and machine keys that wait for your approval, grouped by source machine and reason</li>
      <li><code>approve &lt;ID&gt;</code> — Install the package, or trust the machine key. <code>machine:&lt;id&gt;</code> works as <code>tether machines trust &lt;id&gt;</code></li>
      <li><code>approve --all [--from &lt;MACHINE&gt;]</code> — Approve and install every package in the Inbox, or every package from one machine. It lists the packages and asks first. Without a terminal, it needs <code>-y</code>. It leaves machine keys, malicious packages and packages whose record fails its signature in the Inbox. It approves only the packages it listed</li>
      <li><code>reject &lt;ID&gt;</code> — Stop offering that version, tap, or key</li>
      <li><code>--expect &lt;VERSION|TAP|KEY&gt;</code> — The version, Homebrew tap, or key fingerprint you reviewed, as <code>packages inbox</code> shows it. It is required without a terminal. Tether refuses the decision when the item changed</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The 1.x forms still work: <code>packages remove</code>, and <code>approve &lt;ID&gt; &lt;EXPECTED&gt;</code> without <code>--expect</code>.</p>

    <h3 id="cmd-collab">tether collab</h3>
    <p>Share project secrets with GitHub collaborators (requires write access).</p>
    <Code code={`tether collab init              # Initialize collab for current project
tether collab join <URL>        # Join existing collab
tether collab add <FILE>        # Add secret file (e.g., .env)
tether collab add-project <PATH> # Add another project to a collab
tether collab refresh           # Re-encrypt for current collaborators
tether collab list              # List all collabs
tether collab remove [NAME]     # Remove a collab`} />
  </section>

